<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- When creating a new activity, make a copy of this file with appropriate name -->
<worksheet xml:id="recitation-Matrix-Exponential">
  <title>Matrix Exponential</title>

  <!-- Optional introduction -->
  <introduction>
    <p>
      In Calculus II, you saw the series expansion of the exponential function:
      <md>
        <mrow> e^{at} \amp = 1 + at + \frac{a^2t^2}{2!} + \frac{a^3t^3}{3!} \dotsb = \sum_{k = 0}^{\infty} \frac{a^kt^k}{k!} </mrow>
      </md>
      We can use this formula to expand the definition of <m>e^{at}</m> to involve matrices.
      For an <m>n\times n</m> matrix <m>A</m>:
      <md>
        <mrow> e^{At} \amp = 1 + At + \frac{A^2t^2}{2!} + \frac{A^3t^3}{3!} \dotsb = \sum_{k = 0}^{\infty} \frac{A^kt^k}{k!} </mrow>
      </md>
      Some facts that will feel familiar (although they require justification): (1) <m>\frac{d}{dt}\left( e^{At}\right) = A e^{At}</m>, and (2) the general solution of <m>\vec{x}' = A \vec{x}</m> is <m> \vec{x}(t) = e^{At}\vec{C}, </m> where <m>\vec{C}</m> is a constant column vector.
    </p>
  </introduction>


  <page>
    <exercise>
      <introduction>
        <p>
          Let <m>A = \begin{bmatrix} a \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp b \end{bmatrix}</m>.
        </p>
      </introduction>


      <task workspace="1in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Calculate <m>A^2</m>.
            Calculate <m>A^3</m>.
            Find a nice formula for <m>A^n</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>


      <task workspace="1in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Calculate <m>e^{At}</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>


      <task workspace="1in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Verify that <m>e^{At}\vec{C}</m> is a solution to the system <m>\vec{x}' = A \vec{x}</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
    </exercise>
  </page>


  <page>
    <p>
      The Laplace transform applies to matrix functions, with the rules you're familiar with transferring pretty directly.
      For example, if <m>\vec{x}(t)</m> is a vector function with Laplace transform <m>\L\{\vec{x}(t)\} = X(s)</m>, then consider the initial value problem:
      <md>
        <mrow> \vec{x}' = A \vec{x}, \quad \vec{x}(0) = I </mrow>
      </md>
      Then the solution should be of the form <m>e^{At}\vec{C}</m> for some constant matrix <m>\vec{C}</m>, but <m>e^{At}</m> may be hard to calculate if <m>A</m> is not a diagonal matrix.
      Applying the Laplace transform:
      <md>
        <mrow> s X(s) - \vec{x}(0) = A X(s) \quad \Rightarrow \quad X(s) = (sI - A)^{-1}. </mrow>
      </md>
    </p>

    <exercise>
      <introduction>
        <p>
          Consider the matrix <m>A = \begin{bmatrix} 2 \amp 1 \\ 3 \amp 4 \end{bmatrix}</m>.
        </p>
      </introduction>


      <task workspace="1in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Find <m>X(s) = (sI - A)^{-1}</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>


      <task workspace="1in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Taking the inverse Laplace transform <m>\L^{-1}\{X(s)\}</m> will involve multiple partial fraction decompositions.
            Find <m>\L^{-1}\{X(s)\}</m>.
          </p>

          <p>
            (Feel free to use something like WolframAlpha to save you some tedious work here.)
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
    </exercise>
  </page>
</worksheet>